        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Are treadmill safety keys universal?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. Most home treadmills use a magnetic key, and a universal magnetic key will often work on those, but recesses differ in shape and size, and some treadmills use a pin or insert key that a magnet cannot replace. Match your manual's part number where you can, and test any replacement before running on it.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can I use a regular magnet instead of a treadmill safety key?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. A loose magnet or other object in the slot will let the treadmill run without a key attached to you, which removes the device that stops the belt if you fall. Use a proper key with a cord and clip, and test that pulling it stops the belt.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How do I know if a replacement safety key works?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Stand on the side rails, start the belt at its lowest speed, and pull the key out by its cord. The belt should stop promptly. Repeat several times from different angles. If it fails to stop even once, or only works when pressed into place, do not use it.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What safety key does a NordicTrack treadmill use?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Most recent NordicTrack and ProForm home treadmills use a round magnetic key, which is why many universal magnetic keys name those brands. Some older models used a different key, such as part 160695. Check your manual's parts list or ask the maker with your model and serial number.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Why does my treadmill stop randomly with the key in?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Often the key is not seated firmly, the recess is dirty, the magnet has weakened, or the cord is being tugged by your arm. Clean the recess, reseat the key, check the cord length, and try another key. If two good keys both cut out, the switch behind the console may be faulty.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Where is the safety key on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It sits in a slot or recess on the console, usually near the centre or beside the stop button, with a cord running to a clip. If the slot is empty and the machine shows a key message or will not start, the key is missing or not seated.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

